:::</md></sn><sn><md>### Academic Evolution

In 2018, University of Kentucky researchers published a conference paper comparing the use of rclone and other command-line, cloud data transfer agents for big data. The paper was published as a result of funding by the National Science Foundation.

Later that year, the University of Utah's Center for High Performance Computing examined the impact of rclone options on data transfer rates.


</md></sn><sn><md>### Use in Cybercrime

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-8
In May 2020, reports stated rclone had been used by hackers to exploit Diebold Nixdorf ATMs with ProLock ransomware. The FBI issued a Flash Alert (MI-000125-MW) on May 4, 2020, in relation to the compromise, followed by a related alert (20200901–001) in September 2020. Attackers exfiltrated and encrypted data from organisations involved in healthcare, construction, finance, and legal services, affecting multiple US government agencies and industrial entities. Researchers established that the hackers spent about a month exploring breached networks, using rclone to archive stolen data to cloud storage before encrypting target systems. Reported targets included LaSalle County and the city of Novi Sad.
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-4
![img](https://australiancybersecuritymagazine.com.au/wp-content/uploads/2024/11/The-Federal-Bureau-of-Investigation.jpg)
:::
:::



In January 2021, the FBI warned in Private Industry Notification 20210106–001 of extortion activity using Egregor ransomware and rclone. Organisations worldwide were threatened with the public release of exfiltrated data. In some cases, rclone was disguised under the executable name `svchost`. Reported victims included bookseller Barnes &amp; Noble, US retailer Kmart, video game developer Ubisoft, and the Vancouver metro system.

In April 2021, a cybersecurity investigation into the SonicWall VPN zero-day vulnerability (SNWLID-2021-0001) by FireEye's Mandiant team established that attackers (UNC2447) used rclone for reconnaissance and exfiltration of victims' files. Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency Analysis Report AR21-126A confirmed this use of rclone in FiveHands ransomware attacks.
</md></sn>